1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,337
Take a flat sheet of paper.

2
00:00:01,607 --> 00:00:05,891
You can roll it into a cylinder without stretching it,
and without a single crease.

3
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:09,852
Now bend the cylinder around,
and glue its two ends together.

4
00:00:10,122 --> 00:00:12,316
You get a torus, the surface of a donut.

5
00:00:12,883 --> 00:00:14,142
Here is a harder question.

6
00:00:14,412 --> 00:00:18,839
Can you lay a torus-shaped membrane smoothly
over a sphere, without any creases?

7
00:00:19,109 --> 00:00:19,729
You cannot.

8
00:00:19,999 --> 00:00:22,873
Somewhere, the membrane has
to fold back on itself.

9
00:00:23,433 --> 00:00:25,856
Electrons in a crystal do exactly this.

10
00:00:26,126 --> 00:00:30,166
In a two-band crystal,
every band presses a torus onto a sphere

11
00:00:30,186 --> 00:00:34,419
and hidden in the folds is a topological number,
the Chern number.

12
00:00:34,983 --> 00:00:40,098
This video follows three papers by Shin-Ming Huang,
which read all of this from a

13
00:00:40,118 --> 00:00:45,359
single object, the projector onto the occupied states,
without ever fixing a phase.

14
00:00:46,717 --> 00:00:47,584
Here is the stage.

15
00:00:47,854 --> 00:00:51,518
On the left is the Brillouin zone,
a square of crystal momenta.

16
00:00:51,788 --> 00:00:54,132
Opposite edges describe the same states

17
00:00:54,152 --> 00:00:57,830
so they are glued together,
and the square is really a torus.

18
00:00:58,384 --> 00:01:00,045
On the right is the space of states.

19
00:01:00,315 --> 00:01:04,532
For two bands, a state up to its overall
phase is a point on the Bloch sphere.

20
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:07,299
As the momentum moves, the state moves.

21
00:01:07,569 --> 00:01:11,663
We use one model throughout,
the Qi Wu Zhang model with mass one

22
00:01:11,683 --> 00:01:14,107
the same band as in our earlier video.

23
00:01:14,377 --> 00:01:15,603
Its Chern number is one.

24
00:01:16,167 --> 00:01:18,967
Now paint the whole grid onto
the sphere, cell by cell.

25
00:01:19,237 --> 00:01:22,927
Most of the zone lands one way up,
and we color those cells blue.

26
00:01:23,197 --> 00:01:26,320
But around the point M,
where both momenta equal pi

27
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:29,648
the sheet lands flipped over,
and those cells are red.

28
00:01:30,217 --> 00:01:32,189
Where blue meets red, the map folds.

29
00:01:32,459 --> 00:01:33,651
Keep this picture in mind.

30
00:01:33,921 --> 00:01:37,382
We will come back to it again and again,
adding one layer at a time.

31
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:41,180
The first paper, by Huang and Giataganas

32
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:44,856
starts with a simple question:
how far apart are two states?

33
00:01:45,417 --> 00:01:49,975
A state does not care about its overall phase,
so compare projectors instead.

34
00:01:50,245 --> 00:01:53,116
For one band, two states make an angle theta

35
00:01:53,136 --> 00:01:55,875
whose cosine is the size of their overlap.

36
00:01:56,433 --> 00:02:00,120
On this Bloch sphere of radius one half,
the angle has a picture.

37
00:02:00,390 --> 00:02:03,847
The straight chord between the
two points has length sine theta

38
00:02:03,867 --> 00:02:06,717
the Hilbert Schmidt distance
between the projectors.

39
00:02:06,987 --> 00:02:10,821
The arc along the sphere has length theta,
the Fubini Study distance.

40
00:02:11,091 --> 00:02:12,572
For nearby states the two agree.

41
00:02:13,133 --> 00:02:17,937
With several occupied bands,
the occupied states form a plane rather than a line.

42
00:02:18,207 --> 00:02:20,814
Two planes are separated by a set of angles

43
00:02:20,834 --> 00:02:24,664
the principal angles,
one for each pair of matching directions.

44
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:28,642
They come from the singular
values of the overlap matrix

45
00:02:28,662 --> 00:02:32,805
so they do not change when you rotate
the basis inside either plane.

46
00:02:33,075 --> 00:02:37,408
The Hilbert Schmidt distance is the square
root of the sum of their squared sines.

47
00:02:38,767 --> 00:02:41,319
Now suppose all you are given is a table

48
00:02:41,339 --> 00:02:44,598
the distance between every
pair of sampled momenta.

49
00:02:44,868 --> 00:02:47,631
Can you rebuild the shape of the
states from the table alone?

50
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:51,512
Because the Hilbert Schmidt
distance is a straight-line

51
00:02:51,532 --> 00:02:54,239
distance between matrices,
the answer is yes.

52
00:02:54,509 --> 00:02:57,351
Multidimensional scaling looks for points in

53
00:02:57,371 --> 00:03:00,409
ordinary space whose
distances match the table.

54
00:03:00,967 --> 00:03:01,685
Watch it work.

55
00:03:01,955 --> 00:03:05,321
Start from a random cloud,
and let every pair of points pull

56
00:03:05,341 --> 00:03:08,594
or push like a spring,
until its length matches the table.

57
00:03:08,864 --> 00:03:11,605
Each frame here is one real
step of the algorithm.

58
00:03:12,167 --> 00:03:14,866
For the Chern band, the cloud closes into a

59
00:03:14,886 --> 00:03:17,712
complete sphere,
with an empty cavity inside.

60
00:03:18,267 --> 00:03:21,030
For a trivial band,
with mass three point two

61
00:03:21,050 --> 00:03:24,061
it builds only a small cap
around the north pole.

62
00:03:24,331 --> 00:03:27,767
The cap is fully covered,
with no hole in it, but it never closes.

63
00:03:28,037 --> 00:03:30,934
Whether the shape closes up
is a sign of the topology.

64
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:34,084
But distances alone
cannot tell you how often

65
00:03:34,104 --> 00:03:36,341
each piece is covered, or which way up.

66
00:03:36,611 --> 00:03:38,184
For that, we need to zoom in.

67
00:03:39,550 --> 00:03:40,732
Zoom in on one momentum.

68
00:03:41,002 --> 00:03:45,328
A tiny circle of momenta around it
lands on the sphere as a tiny ellipse.

69
00:03:45,883 --> 00:03:47,741
That ellipse is the quantum metric.

70
00:03:48,011 --> 00:03:51,780
It says how far the state moves for
each direction of step in momentum.

71
00:03:52,050 --> 00:03:54,776
The long axis points where
the state changes fastest.

72
00:03:55,333 --> 00:03:59,496
In the second paper, Huang builds everything
from the derivative of the projector.

73
00:03:59,766 --> 00:04:04,969
The metric is one half the trace of d P times d P,
and no phase is ever chosen.

74
00:04:05,533 --> 00:04:07,697
Now take a tiny square of momenta instead.

75
00:04:07,967 --> 00:04:09,849
It lands as a tiny parallelogram.

76
00:04:10,119 --> 00:04:13,762
Its area is the quantum area,
and it has an orientation

77
00:04:13,782 --> 00:04:15,893
it can land face up, or flipped.

78
00:04:16,450 --> 00:04:19,058
The Berry curvature is
exactly this signed area.

79
00:04:19,328 --> 00:04:23,709
It is positive where the cell lands face up,
negative where it lands flipped

80
00:04:23,729 --> 00:04:27,821
and in our units it is twice the
signed area per unit area in momentum.

81
00:04:28,383 --> 00:04:30,454
Here is a check that this
is the right picture.

82
00:04:30,724 --> 00:04:33,478
The other band sits at the
opposite point of the sphere.

83
00:04:33,748 --> 00:04:37,255
Its parallelogram has the same shape,
so the same metric

84
00:04:37,275 --> 00:04:40,468
but it is flipped,
so the opposite Berry curvature.

85
00:04:41,033 --> 00:04:43,833
Yet the metric of both
bands is that of a sphere

86
00:04:43,853 --> 00:04:46,712
of radius one half,
with Gaussian curvature four.

87
00:04:46,982 --> 00:04:49,767
So the Berry curvature is not
the curvature of the metric.

88
00:04:50,037 --> 00:04:50,990
Keep the two apart.

89
00:04:52,350 --> 00:04:55,052
The derivative of the
projector also tells you how

90
00:04:55,072 --> 00:04:57,882
to travel between states
along the straightest path.

91
00:04:58,152 --> 00:05:00,581
Exponentiate it, and you get a geodesic.

92
00:05:00,851 --> 00:05:03,489
On the Bloch sphere,
geodesics are great circles.

93
00:05:04,050 --> 00:05:07,871
Now carry an arrow around a
triangle made of three geodesics

94
00:05:07,891 --> 00:05:11,199
keeping it as parallel as
the curved surface allows.

95
00:05:11,469 --> 00:05:13,095
When it comes home, it has turned.

96
00:05:13,650 --> 00:05:17,829
The turning angle is the curvature,
four, times the enclosed area

97
00:05:17,849 --> 00:05:20,800
which is just the solid
angle of the triangle.

98
00:05:21,367 --> 00:05:23,208
The quantum states remember the loop too.

99
00:05:23,478 --> 00:05:28,901
Multiply the overlaps around the triangle,
one to two, two to three, three to one.

100
00:05:29,171 --> 00:05:32,079
The phase of this product,
the Bargmann invariant

101
00:05:32,099 --> 00:05:36,441
gives the Berry phase of the triangle,
and it is half of the solid angle.

102
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,207
So the arrow turns by the full solid angle,
while the state picks up half of it.

103
00:05:41,477 --> 00:05:44,105
They are proportional,
but they are not the same thing.

104
00:05:44,667 --> 00:05:47,260
This gives a phase-free way
to compute Berry phases.

105
00:05:47,530 --> 00:05:52,278
Replace a smooth loop by a polygon of
sampled states joined by geodesics.

106
00:05:52,548 --> 00:05:57,253
The polygon's phase converges to the Berry
phase of the loop as you add more points.

107
00:05:58,616 --> 00:05:59,516
Back to the fold.

108
00:05:59,786 --> 00:06:02,016
Draw the metric ellipse at every momentum

109
00:06:02,036 --> 00:06:05,803
like the little circles that show
the distortion of a map projection.

110
00:06:06,366 --> 00:06:08,506
Far from the fold the ellipses are fat.

111
00:06:08,776 --> 00:06:13,612
Near the fold they flatten, and on the fold
itself they collapse into line segments

112
00:06:13,632 --> 00:06:16,946
one direction of motion no
longer moves the state at all.

113
00:06:17,499 --> 00:06:18,668
That is what a fold is.

114
00:06:18,938 --> 00:06:23,409
Fold a clear sheet printed with arrows,
and the folded part comes down mirrored.

115
00:06:23,679 --> 00:06:27,987
Two different momenta land on the same state,
with opposite orientations.

116
00:06:28,549 --> 00:06:31,381
In our model the fold is
a closed curve around M

117
00:06:31,401 --> 00:06:34,054
where the determinant
of the metric vanishes.

118
00:06:34,324 --> 00:06:35,923
Inside it, the sheet is flipped.

119
00:06:36,483 --> 00:06:37,694
Now look at the polar cap.

120
00:06:37,964 --> 00:06:41,401
It is covered three times:
two sheets face up, and one flipped.

121
00:06:41,966 --> 00:06:43,670
So there are two ways to add up area.

122
00:06:43,940 --> 00:06:48,540
Count every sheet, and you get the quantum volume:
one point one nine spheres.

123
00:06:49,099 --> 00:06:51,412
Count with signs, and the flipped sheet

124
00:06:51,432 --> 00:06:54,162
cancels one of the others:
exactly one sphere.

125
00:06:54,432 --> 00:06:56,357
That signed count is the Chern number.

126
00:06:56,627 --> 00:07:00,501
The difference is the extra area
tucked into the folds, which is why

127
00:07:00,521 --> 00:07:04,967
in these units, the quantum volume can
never be smaller than the Chern number.

128
00:07:06,333 --> 00:07:08,301
The third paper asks a deeper question.

129
00:07:08,571 --> 00:07:11,292
What does the curvature of the
metric know about topology?

130
00:07:11,850 --> 00:07:15,888
For a smooth closed surface,
the Gauss Bonnet theorem says that the

131
00:07:15,908 --> 00:07:19,885
total Gaussian curvature is two pi
times the Euler characteristic.

132
00:07:20,155 --> 00:07:21,487
For a sphere that is four pi

133
00:07:21,757 --> 00:07:22,910
for a torus it is zero.

134
00:07:23,466 --> 00:07:28,381
Away from the folds, our metric is a piece of sphere,
with constant curvature four.

135
00:07:28,651 --> 00:07:30,716
Add it up over all the regular sheets

136
00:07:30,736 --> 00:07:34,042
and you get plus two point three
seven eight, times two pi.

137
00:07:34,312 --> 00:07:34,927
Not zero.

138
00:07:35,483 --> 00:07:37,119
The missing piece lives on the fold.

139
00:07:37,389 --> 00:07:40,970
Treated as a crease,
the fold carries a singular curvature

140
00:07:40,990 --> 00:07:45,130
and it contributes minus two point
three seven eight, times two pi.

141
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:49,301
The books balance, and the total is zero,
as it must be for a torus.

142
00:07:49,866 --> 00:07:51,141
Now keep the signs instead.

143
00:07:51,411 --> 00:07:54,431
The flipped sheet subtracts,
three sheets become one

144
00:07:54,451 --> 00:07:58,230
and the signed total curvature is
four pi times the Chern number.

145
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:02,227
These totals do not depend on
how the metric is normalized.

146
00:08:02,497 --> 00:08:06,526
Huang's papers use a metric twice as large,
with sphere curvature two

147
00:08:06,546 --> 00:08:09,225
and find the same two
point three seven eight.

148
00:08:10,583 --> 00:08:12,754
The third paper ends with an open question.

149
00:08:13,024 --> 00:08:16,084
What replaces this picture when
there are more than two bands?

150
00:08:16,354 --> 00:08:18,019
What follows is our own proposal.

151
00:08:18,289 --> 00:08:20,954
It is not yet published,
and it is marked as such.

152
00:08:21,516 --> 00:08:24,772
With a third band,
the space of states has four real

153
00:08:24,792 --> 00:08:28,364
dimensions instead of two,
and a fold has room to escape.

154
00:08:28,634 --> 00:08:32,452
Take the simplest fold,
where s and t go to s and t squared.

155
00:08:32,722 --> 00:08:36,140
Push it gently into a new direction,
epsilon times t

156
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:38,785
and the crease opens into a smooth bend.

157
00:08:39,350 --> 00:08:41,516
Then what replaces the plus or minus sign?

158
00:08:41,786 --> 00:08:43,415
An angle, the Kähler angle.

159
00:08:43,685 --> 00:08:47,950
The Berry curvature is twice its
cosine times the quantum area density.

160
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:51,524
For two bands the cosine can
only be plus or minus one

161
00:08:51,544 --> 00:08:53,309
and it jumps across the fold.

162
00:08:53,579 --> 00:08:55,240
For three bands it varies smoothly.

163
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:58,304
Points where its size
reaches one are special

164
00:08:58,324 --> 00:09:00,660
there the tangent plane is a complex line.

165
00:09:00,930 --> 00:09:04,937
In our three-band example there
are five such complex points

166
00:09:04,957 --> 00:09:08,562
four with index plus one and
one with index minus one.

167
00:09:08,832 --> 00:09:12,334
Their signed count is three,
three times the size of the

168
00:09:12,354 --> 00:09:15,918
Chern number, exactly as a
topological argument predicts.

169
00:09:16,483 --> 00:09:18,764
And since this surface no longer folds

170
00:09:18,784 --> 00:09:21,671
the ordinary Gauss Bonnet
theorem applies again.

171
00:09:21,941 --> 00:09:26,379
The total Gaussian curvature vanishes,
and it no longer carries the Chern number.

172
00:09:27,733 --> 00:09:29,379
Let's put the three papers together.

173
00:09:29,649 --> 00:09:31,757
The first compares states at a distance

174
00:09:31,777 --> 00:09:34,539
and rebuilds their shape
from a table of distances.

175
00:09:35,100 --> 00:09:38,641
The second differentiates the projector,
giving the metric

176
00:09:38,661 --> 00:09:41,588
the Berry curvature,
geodesics and Berry phases.

177
00:09:41,858 --> 00:09:46,531
The same toolbox gives Wilson loops,
winding numbers and the Z two index.

178
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:51,146
The third integrates curvature carefully,
folds and signs included

179
00:09:51,166 --> 00:09:53,919
and gets the Chern number
back from geometry.

180
00:09:54,483 --> 00:09:58,818
All of it starts from one object,
the projector, with no phase to fix

181
00:09:58,838 --> 00:10:04,245
and it all lives in one picture:
a torus pressed onto a sphere, folding where it must.

182
00:10:04,515 --> 00:10:05,374
Thanks for watching.
